1
00:01:46,840 --> 00:01:53,000
The third question we need to ask ourselves
is: what information do readers already know?

2
00:01:53,000 --> 00:02:00,000
This military map of London created by the Soviet
army in the 1980s was designed for use by the that

3
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:05,080
army in the event of an invasion. The people
making this map would’ve known that their

4
00:02:05,080 --> 00:02:09,880
readers would be familiar with things like
the specialist symbols you can see on this

5
00:02:09,880 --> 00:02:16,360
map. Using those symbols means that the map
makers could squeeze in a lot of information

6
00:02:16,360 --> 00:02:22,040
that might not have been possible if the map
was intended for a more general audience.

7
00:02:22,040 --> 00:02:28,440
Understanding what readers already know also
helps us decide what not to include on a map.

8
00:02:28,440 --> 00:02:33,400
If our intended audience can be expected
to already know an area well we might be

9
00:02:33,400 --> 00:02:38,480
able to leave out things that we would
otherwise have included. Leaving things

10
00:02:38,480 --> 00:02:43,280
out might actually make a map better, if
it means it’s easier for the audience to

11
00:02:43,280 --> 00:02:50,400
focus on the main message of the map. But it
depends on the needs of each map audience.

12
00:02:50,400 --> 00:02:56,440
The next question to ask ourselves is: in what
context are people going to use this map? It’s

13
00:02:56,440 --> 00:03:00,920
particularly important to think about whether a
map will be static – for example if it is going

14
00:03:00,920 --> 00:03:07,960
to be printed out or distributed as a PDF –
or dynamic – for example a map on a website.

15
00:03:07,960 --> 00:03:12,200
Dynamic maps can be zoomed in and
out, and users can often choose to

16
00:03:12,200 --> 00:03:16,960
add or remove layers of information,
whereas if you are making a static map

17
00:03:16,960 --> 00:03:22,640
you need to choose the best scale and which
features to include when you make the map.

18
00:03:22,640 --> 00:03:27,760
If your map is going to be printed out, you also
need to think about whether it will still convey

19
00:03:27,760 --> 00:03:33,680
all the necessary information if someone
photocopies the map in black and white.

20
00:03:33,680 --> 00:03:39,000
The final question to ask yourself when
creating a map is: how could this map lead

21
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:45,280
the audience to a wrong conclusion? Maps are
powerful tools for communicating information

22
00:03:45,280 --> 00:03:50,480
about the world, so it’s important to
make sure our maps aren’t misleading.

23
00:03:50,480 --> 00:03:55,400
For example this map shows hotspots
of violent crime in London. You can

24
00:03:55,400 --> 00:03:59,840
see that some of the hotspots
are very small and some are more

25
00:03:59,840 --> 00:04:07,160
diffuse. Humans tend to interpret larger areas
of colour on maps as being more important,

26
00:04:07,160 --> 00:04:12,400
which could be misleading if a large diffuse
hotspot actually had fewer violent crimes

27
00:04:12,400 --> 00:04:18,280
than a smaller but more intense hotspot. To
prevent this, when I made this map I numbered

28
00:04:18,280 --> 00:04:25,440
the hotspots so that readers could clearly
see which places had the most violent crime.

29
00:04:25,440 --> 00:04:29,720
So, in summary, every time you make
a map you should first think about:

30
00:04:30,880 --> 00:04:37,080
What will people use the map for?
What information do readers need?

31
00:04:37,080 --> 00:04:43,960
What information to readers already know?
In what context are people going to use this map?

32
00:04:43,960 --> 00:04:50,320
How could this map lead the
audience to a wrong conclusion?